    kind: work_item  # d290
    title: "Edge.quality field declared on every edge but always None (96864/96864 = 100% null on self-analysis)"  # d290
    status: todo_soft  # d290
    priority: 4  # d290
    parent:  # d290
    tags:  # d290
    - schema  # d290
    - dead_field  # d290
    - edge  # d290
    - self_analysis  # d290
    - dogfooding  # d290
    isbefore: []  # d290
    duplicate_of: []  # d290
    not_duplicate_of: []  # d290
    pr_ref:  # d290
    description: "**Found via self-analysis dogfooding on 2026-05-16 (round 13).**\n\n**Symptom.** On hypergumbo's self-analysis, **every edge** has `quality: None`:\n\n```python\n>>> from collections import Counter\n>>> Counter(e.get('quality') for e in baseline['edges'])\nCounter({None: 96864})\n```\n\n100% of edges. Across all edge types (calls, contains, imports, instantiates, references, dispatches_to, decorated_by, extends, uses, event_publishes, module_exports, depends_on_manifest, depends_on, script_src). Across all origins (python-ast-v1, containment-linker-v1, type-hierarchy-v1, decorator-dispatch-linker-v1, …). The field is declared on the Edge dataclass and serialized into every output, but no producer ever sets a non-None value.\n\n**Why this is a problem.** The `quality` field is documented in the Edge schema as one of several identity/provenance hooks (alongside `origin`, `origin_run_id`, `origin_run_signature`, `confidence`, `is_resolved`). A consumer reading the schema reasonably expects `quality` to indicate something — confidence subtier, resolution quality, evidence strength, etc.\n\nIn practice, the field is informationally identical to \"omitted.\" Costs:\n- Wire size: ~5 bytes × 96,864 edges = ~470 KB of `\"quality\": null,` per behavior map\n- Cognitive: consumers must learn that `quality` is reserved-but-unused versus genuinely informative\n- Schema rot: the longer the field sits empty, the harder it gets to populate (downstream tools have learned to ignore it)\n\nSame pattern as several other findings filed this session:\n- `WI-fanun-...` (round 9): Symbol.fingerprint null on 99.7% of nodes\n- `WI-bujim-...` (round 12): top-level features[] always empty\n- `WI-luvig-...` (round 7): Symbol.canonical_name oversells its scope in docs\n\n**Severity.** Low. Doesn't break consumers, but it's wire-format clutter and schema-trust erosion.\n\n**Fix proposal — two viable approaches.**\n\n1. **Populate `quality`.** If the field is meant to carry e.g. \"high\", \"medium\", \"low\" derived from confidence buckets, write the producer that computes it. Lowest-friction definition: derive from confidence (≥ 0.85 → \"high\", 0.5–0.85 → \"medium\", < 0.5 → \"low\"). Or model resolution-pathway quality from meta. Larger refactor, makes the field useful.\n\n2. **Document the gap or drop the field.** Either:\n   - Add a one-line spec note: \"`quality` is reserved; currently always null.\"\n   - Remove from the Edge dataclass and from to_dict() output.\n\nRecommended: **2 for now, 1 as future work.** If no concrete use case exists, dropping the field is honest. If there's a planned use (e.g., scheduled for a phase 4c per ADR-XXXX), document and keep.\n\n**Test gap.** Add a property test that asserts either `edge.quality is None for all edges` (codifying current state, fails when a producer starts populating) OR `edge.quality in {None, \"high\", \"medium\", \"low\"}` (if populated). Pick one consistent contract.\n\n**Cross-references.**\n\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/ir.py` — Edge dataclass (quality field declaration)\n- `WI-fanun-...` (round 9) — fingerprint dead-field pattern\n- `WI-bujim-...` (round 12) — features[] dead-field pattern\n- `WI-luvig-...` (round 7) — canonical_name docstring oversells\n- `WI-soraj-...` (round 8) — total_files ambiguous semantic, sibling \"schema field semantic gap\""  # d290
    fields: {}  # d290
- op: update  # 8155
  at: '2026-05-17T21:42:20Z'  # 8155
  by: agent  # 8155
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 8155
  clock: 2  # 8155
  nonce: '8155'  # 8155
  set:  # 8155
    description: "**REVISED 2026-05-17 — original kill-the-field framing was wrong.** Investigation found that `Edge.quality` and `Symbol.quality` are spec-declared 🟪 (\"needs design\") placeholders, not vestigial schema rot. The 100% null state is consistent with their spec status.\n\n## Empirical observation (unchanged)\n\nOn hypergumbo's self-analysis, every edge has `quality: None`:\n\n```python\n>>> from collections import Counter\n>>> Counter(e.get('quality') for e in baseline['edges'])\nCounter({None: 96864})\n```\n\n100% of edges, across all edge types and all origins. Same pattern on Symbols: the `Symbol.quality` field at `ir.py:267` is also `Optional[Dict[str, Any]] = None` and likewise unpopulated.\n\n## What the spec actually says\n\nThe field has explicit design intent in `docs/hypergumbo-spec.md`:\n\n- **Line 263** declares it with a 🟪 status marker: `quality: QualityScore # 🟪 QualityScore not defined; code: Optional[Dict[str, Any]] = None`. Per the spec's status legend at line 14, 🟪 means *\"needs design — Requires more design thinking before implementation.\"*\n- **Line 781** specifies the designed sub-shape: *\"**quality.reason** remains for human debugging but is NOT relied upon for programmatic logic.\"* So the designed shape is `quality = {\"score\": ..., \"reason\": \"...\"}` — a structured score plus a human-debug string.\n- **Schema** declares it at `docs/schema.json:407` (Symbol) and `:760` (Edge) as `oneOf: object | null` with description \"Quality assessment.\"\n\n**100% null is the CORRECT current state for a 🟪 field with no producer yet.** Killing the field would erase a designed placeholder and create new spec-vs-data drift.\n\n## Why this is a problem (refined)\n\nThe original framing called this \"wire-format clutter and schema-trust erosion.\" That's empirically accurate but misses the design context. The real problem is:\n\n1. **Spec promises something that doesn't ship.** A reader of the spec sees `quality: QualityScore` and the line about `quality.reason` and reasonably expects production output to have a quality assessment somewhere. They get None.\n2. **🟪 status is stale.** The 🟪 marker is for things that need design thinking *before implementation* — but if nobody is actively designing it, it's stuck in placeholder-purgatory.\n3. **Wire bloat is real but secondary.** ~470 KB of `\"quality\": null,` per behavior map is non-zero cost, but the design decision is the gating concern.\n\n## Two real dispositions (not \"populate vs. drop\" — both are honest)\n\nThe original description framed the choice as \"populate\" vs. \"document/drop.\" Neither alone is right. The honest choices are:\n\n### (a) Advance the design from 🟪 to ⬜/🟨\n\nDefine what `QualityScore` actually is. Plausible shapes the spec/related material hints at:\n- **Tier label**: `{\"score\": \"high\" | \"medium\" | \"low\", \"reason\": \"...\"}` derived from confidence buckets (≥0.85 / 0.5-0.85 / <0.5)\n- **Resolution-pathway label**: `{\"score\": \"type_resolved\" | \"name_only\" | \"fallback\", \"reason\": \"...\"}` mirroring `meta[\"resolution_quality\"]` at the call-construct layer (per `docs/hypergumbo-spec.md:761`)\n- **Composite**: combine confidence-tier with provenance signals\n\nPick at least one producer to fill it. Add the property test contract. Mark 🟪 → 🟩 in the spec.\n\n### (b) Retire from spec\n\nIf quality scoring is no longer planned, edit `docs/hypergumbo-spec.md` to retire `QualityScore` from `:263` AND retire the `quality.reason` sentence at `:781`. THEN drop the field from `Edge`/`Symbol` dataclasses + `to_dict()` + schema. The spec and the data stay consistent.\n\n**Neither (a) nor (b) is \"kill the field.\"** Killing without retiring the spec creates fresh drift; populating without designing creates churn. The design call is the gate.\n\n## Severity (refined)\n\nLow individually. Higher when bundled with other 🟪-or-half-built fields — see the related-items cluster below. The cluster is the actual scope of the design conversation.\n\n## Related (and now structured)\n\nOther fields with similar shape:\n\n- `Symbol.fingerprint` — `WI-fanun`. 0.3% populated (manifest analyzers only). Parented under META `INV-zadum` (write-side identity contract sub-META).\n- `Symbol.canonical_name` — `WI-luvig`. Niche-language analyzers only. Parented under `INV-zadum`.\n- `Symbol.stable_id` — `INV-sotiv`. Missing for 7 kinds + 99.4% of files. Parented under `INV-zadum`.\n- `AnalysisRun.repo_fingerprint` — `INV-tofur`. 100% null but spec-defined algorithm exists at spec `:378-384`.\n- `features[]` — `WI-bujim`. Spec-documented as named slices; implementation diverged to separate files.\n\n**This item (`Edge.quality` / `Symbol.quality`) is NOT under any current META.** It could conceivably join a future \"Quality / uncertainty annotation\" sub-META alongside parts of `INV-zuhub` (the fallback edge contract), but premature with only one item.\n\n## Test gap\n\nAdd a property test that codifies the chosen disposition:\n- (a): once producers exist, assert `edge.quality is None OR quality[\"score\"] in {<defined set>}` for every edge\n- (b): once removed, assert `quality` field is absent from `to_dict()` output\n\nCurrently no test pins the state; producers could silently start writing strings without contract enforcement.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Cross-stage identity-uncertainty axis covered by META `INV-dajaf` (super) — `Edge.quality` is about edge *quality assessment*, which is a different concept from edge *identity* or *resolution status*. Don't conflate."  # 8155
